data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 11 (shipping with .NET 11), the **Cosmos DB provider removes the sync-over-async opt-in entirely**. The workaround you configured in .NET 10 — typically:

```csharp
options.UseCosmos(...).ConfigureWarnings(w =>
    w.Ignore(CosmosEventId.SyncNotSupported));
```

…no longer suppresses the error. All synchronous LINQ operators (`ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`) and `SaveChanges()` now throw **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime.

## Fix: migrate to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

| Sync (now broken) | Async replacement |
|-|-|
| `ToList()` | `ToListAsync()` |
| `First()` | `FirstAsync()` |
| `Count()` | `CountAsync()` |
| `SaveChanges()` | `SaveChangesAsync()` |

The change propagates up the call stack — callers must also become `async`/`await`. If you have middleware or controllers calling these methods synchronously, those need updating too.
